  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nWednesday night through Thursday night\nAssessment...medium confidence (40-50%) confidence on rain occurring.\nLow (<20%) confidence on where rain will occur.\n\nA frontal boundary will vacillate across the area through Thursday \nnight. Both nights the LLJ will increase and help initiate new rounds \nof storms across the area. The signal for this is a given. The \nuncertainty is where each round of showers/storms develops and moves \nduring the overnight hours of Wednesday night and Thursday night.\n\nDuring the day on Thursday, the nocturnal round from Wednesday night \nwill slowly dissipate during the late morning. Thursday afternoon \nlooks to be mainly dry with some isolated diurnal convection \noccurring on boundaries left over from the morning rain.\n\nFriday-Saturday...The mid-level trough moves into the Dakotas and \nMinnesota, dragging the Colorado low with it towards Iowa. A \nWAA wing will likely develop along the warm front moving into \nour CWA and force more scattered storms (50-70% coverage). \nMuggy conditions are likely (60-90%) to return Friday into \nSaturday with dew points back into the upper 60s and 70s \nareawide. By Saturday, the low pressure slowly meanders across\nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley as another round of \nscattered storms (40-70% coverage) is forecast to form from the\nenhanced surface convergence.\n\nSunday-Tuesday...The trough over the western CONUS finally \nejects eastward into the Northern Plain on Sunday, introducing \nthe upper- level flow needed to kick the low pressure sitting \nover the region eastward towards the Great Lakes. With the \nreturn to northerly surface flow/dry air advection on the \nbackside of this low, PoPs should decrease on Sunday with Monday\nlikely (80-95% chance) being the first area-wide dry day in \nover a week as seasonable conditions resume!",
